WEBVTT

0
00:00.170 --> 00:01.230
<v Krista>In this video we're talking about</v>

1
00:01.230 --> 00:04.040
how to find the least common multiple of a set of numbers,

2
00:04.040 --> 00:06.040
and the least common multiple is just the number

3
00:06.040 --> 00:08.040
that both of the numbers we've been given

4
00:08.040 --> 00:09.640
will divide into evenly.

5
00:09.640 --> 00:10.473
So in other words,

6
00:10.473 --> 00:12.410
if we just talk about multiples in general.

7
00:12.410 --> 00:14.780
Here we've been given two numbers, 10 and 15.

8
00:14.780 --> 00:16.690
Let's talk about the multiples of 10.

9
00:16.690 --> 00:18.950
Well, when we talk about the multiples of a number,

10
00:18.950 --> 00:22.960
what we mean is the multiples of 10 will be 10 times one,

11
00:22.960 --> 00:26.800
so 10, 10 times two, 20,

12
00:26.800 --> 00:31.800
10 times three, 30, 10 times four is 40.

13
00:31.940 --> 00:34.720
So in other words, 10, 20, 30, and 40

14
00:34.720 --> 00:36.330
are all multiples of 10.

15
00:36.330 --> 00:38.320
And I could keep going onto infinity.

16
00:38.320 --> 00:42.560
50, 60, 70, 80, 90, 100, 110, 120.

17
00:42.560 --> 00:44.250
Those are all multiples of 10

18
00:44.250 --> 00:47.550
because 10 divides evenly into all of those numbers.

19
00:47.550 --> 00:49.460
I could also look at the multiples of 15.

20
00:49.460 --> 00:52.940
I could say 15 times one gives me 15,

21
00:52.940 --> 00:55.990
15 times two gives me 30,

22
00:55.990 --> 00:59.420
15 times three gives me 45,

23
00:59.420 --> 01:02.830
15 times four gives me 60.

24
01:02.830 --> 01:04.490
And I could keep going, same thing.

25
01:04.490 --> 01:09.100
So 15, 30, 45, and 60 are all multiples of 15.

26
01:09.100 --> 01:11.890
So then when I say I want to find the least common multiple

27
01:11.890 --> 01:13.300
of two numbers, for example,

28
01:13.300 --> 01:16.350
I want to find the least common multiple of 10 and 15,

29
01:16.350 --> 01:17.860
I'm looking for the smallest number

30
01:17.860 --> 01:19.880
that matches between these two sets.

31
01:19.880 --> 01:23.603
And what I can see is that that's going to be 30.

32
01:24.680 --> 01:29.020
30 is the smallest number that 10 and 15 divide into evenly

33
01:29.020 --> 01:32.400
because 30 is a multiple of both 10 and 15,

34
01:32.400 --> 01:34.650
and there's no number that's smaller than 30

35
01:34.650 --> 01:36.810
that 10 and 15 both go into evenly.

36
01:36.810 --> 01:40.090
10 goes into 10 and 20 evenly, but 15 doesn't.

37
01:40.090 --> 01:44.580
15 goes into 15 evenly, but 10 doesn't go into 15 evenly.

38
01:44.580 --> 01:46.770
So 30 is the least common multiple.

39
01:46.770 --> 01:48.280
If I kept going here with 10,

40
01:48.280 --> 01:53.280
I could say 10 times five is 50 and 10 times six is 60.

41
01:53.840 --> 01:58.840
And then I could say that 60 is also a multiple of 10 and 15

42
01:59.450 --> 02:00.950
because it's in both sets,

43
02:00.950 --> 02:02.930
but it's not the least common multiple

44
02:02.930 --> 02:05.270
because 30 is smaller than 60.

45
02:05.270 --> 02:09.680
So 30 is going to be my least common multiple of 10 and 15.

46
02:09.680 --> 02:11.300
Now, what happens when you have a set of numbers

47
02:11.300 --> 02:12.950
that includes more than just two numbers

48
02:12.950 --> 02:14.860
and maybe it's going to be really difficult

49
02:14.860 --> 02:16.960
to write out all of the multiples of each number

50
02:16.960 --> 02:20.020
until you find one that matches across all three sets?

51
02:20.020 --> 02:21.940
Here we need to find the least common multiple

52
02:21.940 --> 02:24.390
of 24, 10, and 15.

53
02:24.390 --> 02:25.790
So how do we do that?

54
02:25.790 --> 02:27.370
Well, the easiest way to do it is

55
02:27.370 --> 02:29.980
to factor all three numbers into their prime factors.

56
02:29.980 --> 02:30.910
So in other words, we're going to do

57
02:30.910 --> 02:32.510
some prime factorization here.

58
02:32.510 --> 02:34.100
Remember, with prime factorization

59
02:34.100 --> 02:35.520
if we have an even number,

60
02:35.520 --> 02:37.780
we know we can divide two into that number,

61
02:37.780 --> 02:41.560
so we'll get two and 12 because two times 12 is 24.

62
02:41.560 --> 02:43.780
Remember also that a prime number is a number

63
02:43.780 --> 02:46.130
that's divisible only by one and itself.

64
02:46.130 --> 02:47.450
So two is a prime number

65
02:47.450 --> 02:50.190
because the only factors of two are one and two.

66
02:50.190 --> 02:52.620
In other words, one and the number itself.

67
02:52.620 --> 02:54.070
12 is not a prime number

68
02:54.070 --> 02:56.970
because I can divide it by one and 12, one and itself,

69
02:56.970 --> 03:00.260
but I can also divide it by three and four and six.

70
03:00.260 --> 03:03.960
So I want to divide this again by two to get two and six.

71
03:03.960 --> 03:06.020
And two is a prime number, but six isn't,

72
03:06.020 --> 03:07.980
so I need to divide six by two again,

73
03:07.980 --> 03:09.640
and I get two and three.

74
03:09.640 --> 03:11.650
So two, two, two, and three,

75
03:11.650 --> 03:13.720
those are all prime numbers here.

76
03:13.720 --> 03:17.120
So I know that I'm done with my prime factorization,

77
03:17.120 --> 03:19.830
and now I just need to go ahead and write these out.

78
03:19.830 --> 03:23.070
Two times two times two times three.

79
03:23.070 --> 03:25.350
And this is the product of primes of 24.

80
03:25.350 --> 03:27.750
I need to do the same thing for 10 and 15.

81
03:27.750 --> 03:30.810
So I can divide 10 into two and five.

82
03:30.810 --> 03:33.500
Two and five are both prime numbers,

83
03:33.500 --> 03:36.350
so I can't go any further with my prime factorization.

84
03:36.350 --> 03:39.360
And my product primes then is two times five.

85
03:39.360 --> 03:43.130
For 15, I'll divide that into three and five

86
03:43.130 --> 03:44.990
because three times five is 15.

87
03:44.990 --> 03:47.360
Three and five are both prime numbers,

88
03:47.360 --> 03:49.570
so I know I'm done with my prime factorization.

89
03:49.570 --> 03:52.240
And my product of primes is three and five.

90
03:52.240 --> 03:54.790
Now, the only number for which I have repeating factors

91
03:54.790 --> 03:56.990
in my product of primes is this 24 here.

92
03:56.990 --> 03:58.220
I have three factors of two,

93
03:58.220 --> 04:02.310
so I want to rewrite this as two cubed times three.

94
04:02.310 --> 04:05.220
So here I have three factors of two and one factor of three.

95
04:05.220 --> 04:08.210
Here I have one factor of two and one factor of five.

96
04:08.210 --> 04:11.260
And here I have one factor of three and one factor of five.

97
04:11.260 --> 04:13.360
So when I want to find the least common multiple

98
04:13.360 --> 04:15.240
now that I have my prime factors,

99
04:15.240 --> 04:18.720
what I need to do is take the largest number of each factor.

100
04:18.720 --> 04:22.100
So what I see first of all is that I have factors of two

101
04:22.100 --> 04:23.820
and I have factors of two,

102
04:23.820 --> 04:27.720
I have factors of three and factors of three,

103
04:27.720 --> 04:31.350
and I have factors of five and factors of five.

104
04:31.350 --> 04:34.120
So I have to look at two, I have to look at three,

105
04:34.120 --> 04:35.360
and I have to look at five.

106
04:35.360 --> 04:36.640
So first looking at two,

107
04:36.640 --> 04:39.060
what's the largest number of factors that I have for two?

108
04:39.060 --> 04:41.430
Well, in this number I have three factors of two.

109
04:41.430 --> 04:43.720
In this number I have one factor of two.

110
04:43.720 --> 04:46.840
I have to take the larger number of factors of two,

111
04:46.840 --> 04:49.220
so I have to take three factors of two.

112
04:49.220 --> 04:50.900
So I'll say two cubed.

113
04:50.900 --> 04:52.610
Now I look at my factors of three.

114
04:52.610 --> 04:55.660
I have one factor of three, and I have one factor of three.

115
04:55.660 --> 04:58.520
Those are equal, so I just have to take one factor of three.

116
04:58.520 --> 05:00.370
And then I have to look at my factors of five.

117
05:00.370 --> 05:03.010
I have one factor of five and one factor of five,

118
05:03.010 --> 05:05.140
so I have to take one factor of five.

119
05:05.140 --> 05:08.120
And now I can just multiply all of these together

120
05:08.120 --> 05:09.660
to get my least common multiple.

121
05:09.660 --> 05:13.391
So I'm going to say two cubed or two times two times two.

122
05:13.391 --> 05:14.570
That's going to give me eight.

123
05:14.570 --> 05:16.810
Three times five is going to give me 15.

124
05:16.810 --> 05:18.940
So I'm going to say eight times 15.

125
05:18.940 --> 05:22.130
Eight times 15 is going to give me 120,

126
05:22.130 --> 05:25.840
so 120 is going to be the least common multiple

127
05:25.840 --> 05:28.160
of 24, 10, and 15.

128
05:28.160 --> 05:30.920
And I can prove to myself that 120 is a multiple

129
05:30.920 --> 05:34.040
of 24, 10, and 15 by dividing 120

130
05:34.040 --> 05:35.270
by all three of these numbers.

131
05:35.270 --> 05:39.510
So if I divide 120 by 24, I get five.

132
05:39.510 --> 05:43.030
If I divide 120 by 10, I get 12.

133
05:43.030 --> 05:46.690
If I divide 120 by 15, I get eight.

134
05:46.690 --> 05:51.380
So I know that 24, 10, and 15 all go evenly into 120.

135
05:51.380 --> 05:52.740
That helps me double check at least

136
05:52.740 --> 05:54.450
that I do have a multiple

137
05:54.450 --> 05:56.180
of the three numbers I started with,

138
05:56.180 --> 05:58.070
but this is the method you want to use

139
05:58.070 --> 05:59.810
to find the least common multiple

140
05:59.810 --> 06:01.510
of a set of numbers like this one.